{"authored" [{"question" "q-335465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For every neighborhood of an equilibrium, all trajectories starting sufficiently close remain inside that neighborhood for all future time. Non-equilibrium trajectories starting arbitrarily close do not converge to the equilibrium as time increases; they may oscillate or circle around it. How should the equilibrium be classified?\n\nHint: Use the local neighborhood definition of stability, then check whether nearby trajectories converge." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/review.json" "file_sha256" "f4769dfa18ac495e07731d6fd0bcb7bba11417c4ef34431e18cc0bf253b0a444" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "00f241ddeabd9e674d154cfc279d70492b0677592837310cd2472137fd097369" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/verification.json" "basis" 2346 "rationale" "Make original canonical local neighborhood definition explicit, preserving author accepted choice a confirmed by authentic revealed worked solution. Original boundedness wording alone was ambiguous; no source key change. The wording-only clarification preserves the original field and every original answer ID."} {"question" "q-335465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Stable but not asymptotically stable.\"\n\nLocal stability clarification: the hypothesis applies to every neighborhood: for each $\\varepsilon>0$, some $\\delta>0$ ensures that $\\|x(t_0)-x^*\\|<\\delta$ implies $\\|x(t)-x^*\\|<\\varepsilon$ for all $t\\ge t_0$. This establishes stability. Since non-equilibrium trajectories starting arbitrarily close fail to converge, no attracting neighborhood exists, so the equilibrium is not asymptotically stable. The correct choice remains Stable but not asymptotically stable. A single fixed bounded region alone would not imply stability; the clarified wording makes the original lesson definition explicit." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/review.json" "file_sha256" "f4769dfa18ac495e07731d6fd0bcb7bba11417c4ef34431e18cc0bf253b0a444" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "00f241ddeabd9e674d154cfc279d70492b0677592837310cd2472137fd097369" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/verification.json" "basis" 2346 "rationale" "Make original canonical local neighborhood definition explicit, preserving author accepted choice a confirmed by authentic revealed worked solution. Original boundedness wording alone was ambiguous; no source key change. The wording-only clarification preserves the original field and every original answer ID."} {"question" "q-335465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Stable but not asymptotically stable."] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/review.json" "file_sha256" "f4769dfa18ac495e07731d6fd0bcb7bba11417c4ef34431e18cc0bf253b0a444" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "00f241ddeabd9e674d154cfc279d70492b0677592837310cd2472137fd097369" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-335465/verification.json" "basis" 2346 "rationale" "Make original canonical local neighborhood definition explicit, preserving author accepted choice a confirmed by authentic revealed worked solution. Original boundedness wording alone was ambiguous; no source key change. The wording-only clarification preserves the original field and every original answer ID."} {"question" "q-335646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335646" "field" "selection" "attribute"