ue" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "478e0ffeeb616cde990750ee6d12db861d4e636d27e873f81d582bbc202ae000" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" 