 [:answer.type/math "t\\to 0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335458" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "t\\to \\infty "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-335458" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t\\to \\infty "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/state.json" "file_sha256" "746d5e046d23d4ad06c4544810f7e0f65c91c067618bc9f939723cad8012347f" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21665.json" "file_sha256" "a6913b5f1ad1f454fcf7c4244ca05b7a166777f9e1b3e63d791ddb9e6994809e" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/assets/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21665.json" "file_sha256" "a6913b5f1ad1f454fcf7c4244ca05b7a166777f9e1b3e63d791ddb9e6994809e" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n- Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21666.json" "file_sha256" "6c293d74cb2e4a164182899e80e96891dc2f7b0f62079a12532760f37ab808a3" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/example-21666.json" "file_sha256" "6c293d74cb2e4a164182899e80e96891dc2f7b0f62079a12532760f37ab808a3" "basis" 2345 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"q-332968" [] "q-335458" [] "q-335639" [] "q-335646" [] "q-335470" [] "q-335558" [] "q-335460" [] "q-333385" [] "e-21666" [] "e-21665" [] "q-335465" [] "q-333360" []} "basis" 2346 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186220337 17592186220338 17592186220339 17592186220340 17592186220341 17592186359437 17592186359438 17592186359439 17592186359440 17592186359441 17592186359443 17592186359444 17592186359445 17592186359446 17592186359447 17592186359450 17592186359451 17592186359453 17592186359454 17592186359455 17592186359457 17592186359458 17592186359460 17592186359461 17592186359464 17592186359465 17592186359467 17592186359468 17592186359470 17592186359471 17592186359474 17592186359475 17592186359476 17592186359477 17592186359478 17592186359481 17592186359482 17592186359483 17592186359484 17592186359485 17592186359488 17592186359489 17592186359490 17592186359491 17592186359492 17592186359495 17592186359496 17592186359497 17592186359498 17592186359499 17592186359502 17592186359503 17592186359504 17592186359505 17592186359506] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220336 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220336 :answer-field/id] [17592186220336 :answer-field/key] [17592186220336 :answer-field/type] [17592186359436 :answer-field/correct] [17592186359436 :answer-field/id] [17592186359436 :answer-field/key] [17592186359436 :answer-field/type] [17592186359442 :answer-field/correct] [17592186359442 :answer-field/id] [17592186359442 :answer-field/key] [17592186359442 :answer-field/type] [17592186359449 :answer-field/correct] [17592186359449 :answer-field/id] [17592186359449 :answer-field/key] [17